## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical low-level memory management with thread-safety using atomic bit ops on a compact bitmap. A beginner-friendly yet realistic task for systems work.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size block pools
- Bitmap-based tracking
- Atomic CAS and memory order
- Alignment and cache-friendly layout

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stddef.h>

typedef struct {
  alignas(64) unsigned char arena[4096];
  atomic_uint64_t used;
} Pool;

void* allocate_block(Pool* p) {
  uint64_t oldv = atomic_load_explicit(&p->used, memory_order_relaxed);
  while (1) {
    uint64_t free = ~oldv;
    if (!free) return NULL;
    int idx = __builtin_ctzll(free);
    uint64_t want = oldv | (1ull << idx);
    if (atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->used, &oldv, want))
      return (void*)(p->arena + idx * 64);
  }
}

void free_block(Pool* p, void* ptr) {
  size_t idx = ((unsigned char*)ptr - p->arena) / 64;
  uint64_t mask = ~(1ull << idx);
  atomic_fetch_and_explicit(&p->used, mask, memory_order_release);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect double-free? 
- How would you extend to multiple arenas or resizing?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests lock-free design, ABA avoidance with sequence counters, memory ordering, and NUMA-aware reclamation strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free queues with sequence counters
- Epoch-based memory reclamation
- NUMA locality and cache-line padding
- Memory order: acquire/release and fences
- Batch enqueues to reduce barrier overhead

## Code Example
```cpp
// Pseudo-enqueue (simplified)
struct Slot { uint64_t seq; char data[128]; };
Slot ring[N];
_Atomic uint64_t head=0, tail=0;
void enqueue(void* msg){
  uint64_t pos = atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1);
  Slot& s = ring[pos & (N-1)];
  while (s.seq != pos) _mm_pause();
  memcpy(s.data, msg, 128);
  atomic_store_explicit(&s.seq, pos+1, memory_order_release);
}
```

